From: jomon <jomon@local>
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] platform/x86: toshiba_acpi: drain INFO FIFO on each hotkey Notify

On info_supported machines, toshiba_acpi_process_hotkeys() reads the EC
event FIFO (the INFO method) only ONCE per SCI Notify, then returns. The
HCI_SYSTEM_EVENT path, by contrast, loops until TOS_FIFO_EMPTY.

The EC enqueues a press entry (0x14x) and a companion/release entry (0x1Cx)
for every physical hotkey, and may coalesce multiple keys into fewer Notify
events. Reading a single entry per Notify leaves stale entries in the ring
that are replayed on a later keypress, causing hotkey functions to "bleed"
into one another / persist across presses (e.g. pressing brightness-up is
interpreted as brightness-down for the next few presses).

Fix by draining the entire FIFO on each Notify (loop until INFO returns 0,
i.e. the ring is empty), matching the semantics of the HCI_SYSTEM_EVENT
drain. All FIFO codes on affected hardware are unique per key, so draining
cannot drop a legitimate event.

Verified on a Dynabook Portege X40-K: rapid mixed brightness Up/Down and
keyboard-backlight toggle presses now behave correctly with no lag or
cross-talk.

Signed-off-by: jomon <jomon@local>
---
 drivers/platform/x86/toshiba_acpi.c | 18 ++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/platform/x86/toshiba_acpi.c b/drivers/platform/x86/toshiba_acpi.c
index 2222222..3333333 100644
--- a/drivers/platform/x86/toshiba_acpi.c
+++ b/drivers/platform/x86/toshiba_acpi.c
@@ -2822,10 +2822,20 @@ static void toshiba_acpi_process_hotkeys(struct toshiba_acpi_dev *dev)
 {
 	if (dev->info_supported) {
-		int scancode = toshiba_acpi_query_hotkey(dev);
-
-		if (scancode < 0) {
-			pr_err("Failed to query hotkey event\n");
-		} else if (scancode != 0) {
+		/*
+		 * Drain the entire INFO FIFO on each SCI Notify. The EC may
+		 * enqueue a press (0x14x) and a companion/release (0x1Cx)
+		 * entry per physical key, and may coalesce multiple keys
+		 * into fewer Notify events. Reading only one entry per
+		 * Notify leaves stale entries in the ring that get replayed
+		 * on a later keypress (key functions "bleeding" into each
+		 * other / persisting across presses). Loop until INFO
+		 * returns 0 (ring empty), matching the HCI_SYSTEM_EVENT
+		 * path's TOS_FIFO_EMPTY drain semantics.
+		 */
+		for (;;) {
+			int scancode = toshiba_acpi_query_hotkey(dev);
+
+			if (scancode < 0) {
+				pr_err("Failed to query hotkey event\n");
+				break;
+			}
+			if (scancode == 0)
+				break;	/* FIFO empty */
+
 			toshiba_acpi_report_hotkey(dev, scancode);
 			dev->key_event_valid = 1;
 			dev->last_key_event = scancode;
-		}
+		}
 	} else if (dev->system_event_supported) {
